Title: Keep uv__hrtime() from running backwards on Windows XP
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

The first full run of the standalone build on XP -- node.exe from the release
.zip, in a four-processor VM -- died in two places with the same message:

  Assertion failed: new_time >= loop->time, file src\win\core.c, line 327

once in a script that started several workers, and once running `npm -v`.

That assert is uv_update_time() checking that a loop's clock has not gone
backwards, and the clock is uv__hrtime(), which is QueryPerformanceCounter and
nothing else. From Vista on that counter is monotonic, as documented. On XP it
need not be: with more than one processor the HAL may read each processor's own
time stamp counter, those are not synchronised with one another, and a thread
moved from one processor to another between two readings can see the second
come out lower than the first. Multi-core AMD processors are where it was best
known -- "/usepmtimer" in boot.ini exists for it -- and virtual machines show
it too. So this is XP behaving as XP does, not a fault in the build, and any
libuv program can hit it there.

The fix is in uv__hrtime() rather than at the assert, because the assert is
right to want what it wants and it is not the only caller: uv_hrtime() feeds
process.hrtime() and performance.now(), which have no business going backwards
either. Each reading is clamped to the largest one any thread has been given so
far, with an interlocked compare-exchange, so no lock and no allocation. Where
the counter is well behaved the clamp never changes a value. Where it is not,
time stands still for the length of the skew.

InterlockedCompareExchange64 is a compiler intrinsic on both architectures
(cmpxchg8b on ia32), not an import -- kernel32 only began exporting it with
Vista, which would otherwise make this a patch that fixes XP and cannot load
there.

For both builds: Electron runs the same libuv loop on the same systems.

Setting /usepmtimer on an affected machine remains the better cure where it is
available, since it corrects the counter for every program rather than
papering over it in one.

diff --git a/deps/uv/src/win/util.c b/deps/uv/src/win/util.c
--- a/deps/uv/src/win/util.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/win/util.c
@@ -78,6 +78,10 @@
 /* Frequency of the high-resolution clock. */
 static uint64_t hrtime_frequency_ = 0;
 
+/* The highest performance counter reading any thread has been given. See
+ * uv__hrtime(). */
+static volatile LONG64 hrtime_last_ = 0;
+
 /* Parameters for WSAQUERYSETW inside uv__gethostnamew_nt60 */
 static GUID guid_host_name = SVCID_HOSTNAME;
 static AFPROTOCOLS af_protocols[2] = { {AF_INET, IPPROTO_UDP},
@@ -489,6 +493,8 @@
 
 uint64_t uv__hrtime(unsigned int scale) {
   LARGE_INTEGER counter;
+  LONG64 now;
+  LONG64 last;
   double scaled_freq;
   double result;
 
@@ -499,12 +505,44 @@
   }
   assert(counter.QuadPart != 0);
 
+  /* Never hand out a reading lower than one already handed out.
+   *
+   * QueryPerformanceCounter is documented as monotonic and from Vista on it
+   * is. On Windows XP it need not be: with more than one processor the HAL
+   * may read each processor's own time stamp counter, those are not
+   * synchronised, and a thread that is moved from one processor to another
+   * between two calls can see the counter step backwards. It is common on
+   * multi-core AMD processors, which is what "/usepmtimer" in boot.ini was
+   * for, and common in virtual machines.
+   *
+   * libuv is not written for that. uv_update_time() asserts that a loop's
+   * clock has not gone backwards, so the first backward step ends the process
+   * with "Assertion failed: new_time >= loop->time" -- seen on XP running
+   * nothing more than `npm -v`.
+   *
+   * So the reading is clamped to the largest one seen so far, process-wide
+   * and lock-free. Where the counter behaves this never changes a value. Where
+   * it does not, time stands still for the length of the skew instead of
+   * running backwards, which every caller can live with.
+   */
+  now = counter.QuadPart;
+  /* Stores `now` only on the very first call, and otherwise just reads. */
+  last = InterlockedCompareExchange64(&hrtime_last_, now, 0);
+  while (last != 0 && last < now) {
+    LONG64 seen = InterlockedCompareExchange64(&hrtime_last_, now, last);
+    if (seen == last)
+      break;
+    last = seen;
+  }
+  if (last > now)
+    now = last;
+
   /* Because we have no guarantee about the order of magnitude of the
    * performance counter interval, integer math could cause this computation
    * to overflow. Therefore we resort to floating point math.
    */
   scaled_freq = (double) hrtime_frequency_ / scale;
-  result = (double) counter.QuadPart / scaled_freq;
+  result = (double) now / scaled_freq;
   return (uint64_t) result;
 }
 
